[section:wavelet_denoise Wavelet Denoising]

[h4 Synopsis]

    #include <boost/math/filters/wavelet_denoise.hpp>

    namespace boost::math::filters {

    // An orthonormal scaling filter with its boundary rows on an interval.
    template <class Real>
    class orthogonal_wavelet {
    public:
        orthogonal_wavelet();                  // invalid, 0 vanishing moments
        unsigned vanishing_moments() const;
    };

    namespace wavelet {
    template <class Real> orthogonal_wavelet<Real> daubechies(unsigned p);  // p = 2, ..., 10
    template <class Real> orthogonal_wavelet<Real> symlet(unsigned p);      // p = 2, ..., 10, least asymmetric
    }

    enum class threshold_rule { wiener, universal_soft, sure, eb_posterior_median };

    // Fixed wavelet and rule; sigma = NaN estimates the noise level.
    template <class RandomAccessContainer>
    RandomAccessContainer wavelet_denoise(const RandomAccessContainer& x,
                                          const orthogonal_wavelet<typename RandomAccessContainer::value_type>& w,
                                          threshold_rule rule = threshold_rule::wiener,
                                          typename RandomAccessContainer::value_type sigma = quiet_NaN);

    // Wavelet and rule chosen by cross-validation.
    template <class RandomAccessContainer>
    RandomAccessContainer wavelet_denoise(const RandomAccessContainer& x);

    // The coefficients [a_L | d_L | ... | d_1] of x in the basis w, and back; levels: all that n allows by default.
    template <class RandomAccessContainer>
    RandomAccessContainer wavelet_transform(const RandomAccessContainer& x,
                                            const orthogonal_wavelet<typename RandomAccessContainer::value_type>& w,
                                            std::size_t levels = std::size_t(-1));  // -1: all
    template <class RandomAccessContainer>
    RandomAccessContainer inverse_wavelet_transform(const RandomAccessContainer& c,
                                                    const orthogonal_wavelet<typename RandomAccessContainer::value_type>& w,
                                                    std::size_t levels = std::size_t(-1));  // -1: all

    // Whichever of wavelet_denoise(x) and trend_filter(x) has the lower cross-validation score.
    template <class RandomAccessContainer>
    RandomAccessContainer denoise(const RandomAccessContainer& x);

    } // namespaces

    #include <boost/math/filters/symlets.hpp>

    template <typename Real, unsigned p>
    constexpr std::array<Real, 2*p> symlet_scaling_filter();   // p = 2, ..., 10

[h4 Description]

Wavelet shrinkage denoises equally spaced samples of a function on an interval: the detail coefficients of the samples
in an orthonormal wavelet basis are shrunk towards zero, which removes most of the white noise and keeps jumps and kinks.
The basis is on the interval, not periodized: Daubechies or least-asymmetric rows in the interior, and orthogonal boundary rows,
fitted to those of Cohen, Daubechies and Vial (1993), for which polynomials of degree below /p/ have no detail coefficients.
Any /n/ >= 4/p/ - 1 samples work (a level of length /n/[sub /l/] gives [lceil]/n/[sub /l/]\/2[rceil] scaling coefficients); at a level
of odd length the right edge has /p/ - 1 wavelet rows instead of /p/.

[h4 The transform]

`wavelet_transform(x, w)` returns the coefficients of /x/ in the orthonormal basis /w/ as one vector of the same length,
coarsest first: \[/a/[sub /L/] | /d/[sub /L/] | /d/[sub /L/-1] | ... | /d/[sub 1]\], where /d/[sub 1] are the finest details.
Level /l/ takes /n/[sub /l/-1] values to [lceil]/n/[sub /l/-1]\/2[rceil] scaling and [lfloor]/n/[sub /l/-1]\/2[rfloor] wavelet coefficients, /n/[sub 0] = /n/,
so /d/[sub /l/] is the slice \[/n/[sub /l/], /n/[sub /l/-1]). By default /L/ is as large as /n/ allows, a level running while its input has at least 4/p/ - 1 values:
/L/ = `std::bit_width((n - 1) / (4*p - 2))` (in Python, `((n - 1) // (4*p - 2)).bit_length()`);
a smaller `levels` stops early, and `levels = 0` returns /x/. The basis is orthonormal, so the transform preserves energy,
and `inverse_wavelet_transform(c, w, levels)` with the same `levels` recovers /x/ to a few ulps.
Multiprecision types work too (tested with `cpp_bin_float_50`), but the filter tables have about 75 significant digits,
so a wider type is accurate to about 10[super -75], not to its own epsilon.

    auto w = wavelet::symlet<double>(8);
    std::vector<double> c = wavelet_transform(x, w);       // all levels
    std::vector<double> c2 = wavelet_transform(x, w, 2);   // the two finest levels: [a_2 | d_2 | d_1]
    std::vector<double> y = inverse_wavelet_transform(c, w);

[h4 Threshold rules]

The noise level [sigma] is the median absolute value of the finest detail coefficients divided by [Phi][super -1](3/4) = 0.6745, unless it is given.
Every rule leaves the scaling coefficients alone. With /n/ the number of samples and /d/ the detail coefficients of one level:

* `wiener` (the default): the empirical Wiener filter in the wavelet domain (Ghael, Sayeed and Baraniuk 1997):
  each detail coefficient is multiplied by [theta][super 2] / ([theta][super 2] + [sigma][super 2]), [theta] the coefficient of a pilot estimate,
  universal hard thresholding in the first of sym8, db4, sym4 and db2 that differs from /w/ and takes /n/ samples (else /w/ itself).
  It has the lowest error on most signals: with sym8 on Blocks, Bumps, HeaviSine and Doppler at signal-to-noise ratios 7 and 15, 20-30% below the next best rule.
* `universal_soft`: soft thresholding at [sigma] [radic](2 ln /n/) (VisuShrink). It removes nearly every detail, which is best for
  smooth signals (a cubic, say) and up to 12 times worse than `wiener` on signals with jumps or spikes.
* `sure`: SureShrink (Donoho and Johnstone 1995), soft thresholding at the level's minimizer of Stein's unbiased risk estimate,
  or at [sigma] [radic](2 ln /m/) for sparse levels.
* `eb_posterior_median`: the empirical-Bayes posterior median of Johnstone and Silverman (2004, 2005), level by level,
  with a Laplace prior of fixed scale /a/ = 1/2 (EbayesThresh's default) mixed with an atom at zero; the mixing weight is the marginal maximum-likelihood one,
  bounded below by the weight whose threshold is the universal one. It matches R's `EbayesThresh` (`ebayesthresh(x, prior = "laplace", a = 0.5)`)
  to rounding. It adapts to the sparsity of each level.

[h4 Choosing the wavelet and the rule]

The one-argument `wavelet_denoise` chooses among db2, ..., db10, sym4, ..., sym10 (sym2 and sym3 are db2 and db3) and the four rules
by two-fold cross-validation on the even- and odd-indexed samples (Nason 1996), as `trend_filter` does:
each half is denoised, the other half is predicted by averaging neighbouring values, and the squared prediction error is minimized.
With an odd number of samples the even-indexed half has the extra one. Wavelets that do not take both halves are skipped,
so the cross-validated functions need at least 14 samples.

`denoise` returns whichever of the cross-validated `wavelet_denoise` and the cross-validated `trend_filter`
has the lower cross-validation score; if the trend filter fails, the wavelet result is returned. Wavelets win on smooth signals with isolated singularities, the trend filter
on piecewise constant or piecewise linear ones.

    #include <boost/math/filters/wavelet_denoise.hpp>
    #include <cmath>
    #include <random>
    #include <vector>

    using namespace boost::math::filters;
    std::mt19937_64 gen(42);
    std::normal_distribution<double> noise(0, 0.1);
    std::vector<double> y(2048);
    for (std::size_t i = 0; i < y.size(); ++i) {
        double t = double(i) / y.size();
        y[i] = std::sin(4 * M_PI * t) + 2 * t * t + (t > 0.6 ? 0.5 : 0) + noise(gen);
    }
    auto x1 = wavelet_denoise(y, wavelet::symlet<double>(8));                 // sym8, Wiener
    auto x2 = wavelet_denoise(y, wavelet::daubechies<double>(4), threshold_rule::sure, 0.1);
    auto x3 = wavelet_denoise(y);                                             // wavelet and rule by CV
    auto x4 = denoise(y);                                                     // wavelet CV or trend filter, by CV score

[h4 Symlets]

`symlet_scaling_filter<Real, p>()` returns the least-asymmetric Daubechies filter with /p/ vanishing moments, with 75 significant digits,
in the orientation of `daubechies_scaling_filter`. Of the 2[super /k/] spectral factorizations (one root from each reciprocal pair),
it is the one whose phase is closest to linear in least squares over [0, [pi]]. For /p/ = 7 and 10 this is a different and more symmetric filter than
PyWavelets' sym7 and sym10; for the other /p/ the filters agree with PyWavelets'. For /p/ = 2, 3 the symlet is the Daubechies filter.

[h4 Errors]

A __domain_error is raised if /p/ is outside [2, 10], if the input has fewer than 4/p/ - 1 samples (14 for the cross-validated
functions), if [sigma] is negative or infinite, if the rule is unknown, if `levels` exceeds what /n/ allows, or if the wavelet is default-constructed. An __evaluation_error is raised if the boundary rows cannot be generated. When the policy does not throw, the returned container is filled with
the error handler's result.

[h4 Accuracy and Complexity]

The transform is orthogonal to a few [epsilon] for every family and length. `float` data is processed in `double`, as by `trend_filter`;
other types are processed natively. The transform costs /O/(/pn/). Boundary rows are generated on first use (about a millisecond in double)
and kept per wavelet: those of all /n/ = /m/ 2[super /L/] and of the 16 most recently used other parity sequences. The cross-validated
functions cost about 200 transforms of /n/\/2 samples, plus the trend filter for `denoise`; see `reporting/performance/wavelet_denoise_performance.cpp`.

[heading References]

* A. Cohen, I. Daubechies and P. Vial, ['Wavelets on the interval and fast wavelet transforms], Applied and Computational Harmonic Analysis 1 (1993), 54-81, [@https://doi.org/10.1006/acha.1993.1005 doi:10.1006/acha.1993.1005].
* I. M. Johnstone and B. W. Silverman, ['Needles and straw in haystacks: empirical Bayes estimates of possibly sparse sequences], Annals of Statistics 32 (2004), 1594-1649, [@https://doi.org/10.1214/009053604000000030 doi:10.1214/009053604000000030].
* I. M. Johnstone and B. W. Silverman, ['Empirical Bayes selection of wavelet thresholds], Annals of Statistics 33 (2005), 1700-1752, [@https://doi.org/10.1214/009053605000000345 doi:10.1214/009053605000000345].
* D. L. Donoho and I. M. Johnstone, ['Adapting to unknown smoothness via wavelet shrinkage], Journal of the American Statistical Association 90 (1995), 1200-1224, [@https://doi.org/10.1080/01621459.1995.10476626 doi:10.1080/01621459.1995.10476626].
* S. G. Chang, B. Yu and M. Vetterli, ['Adaptive wavelet thresholding for image denoising and compression], IEEE Transactions on Image Processing 9 (2000), 1532-1546, [@https://doi.org/10.1109/83.862633 doi:10.1109/83.862633].
* S. P. Ghael, A. M. Sayeed and R. G. Baraniuk, ['Improved wavelet denoising via empirical Wiener filtering], Proceedings of SPIE 3169 (1997), 389-399, [@https://doi.org/10.1117/12.292799 doi:10.1117/12.292799].
* G. P. Nason, ['Wavelet shrinkage using cross-validation], Journal of the Royal Statistical Society B 58 (1996), 463-479, [@https://doi.org/10.1111/j.2517-6161.1996.tb02094.x doi:10.1111/j.2517-6161.1996.tb02094.x].
* I. Daubechies, ['Ten Lectures on Wavelets], SIAM (1992), section 8.1.

